14. The failure case
Now let's break the system.
We have:
Client
   |
   v
LB
   |
   v
Backend A
Client sends:
GET /orders/123
The LB forwards it to A.
Then:
Backend A crashes
What happens?
It depends on exactly where the failure occurs.
Case 1 — Backend dies before request
LB can potentially detect failure and route future connections elsewhere.
A ❌
B ✓
C ✓

new connection → B
Easy.
Case 2 — Backend dies while processing
Client
   |
   v
LB
   |
   v
A
   |
   X
 crash
Now what?
The LB may detect that the backend connection failed.
But should it automatically send the same request to B?
Danger.
Suppose the request was:
POST /payments
Maybe A successfully processed the payment:
Payment charged ✓
and then crashed before responding.
LB doesn't necessarily know whether the business operation succeeded.
If it blindly retries:
POST /payments
against B:
Payment charged AGAIN ❌
This is exactly why load balancing is connected to:
Idempotency
Retry
Exactly-once vs at-least-once
Distributed transactions
Those are later modules in your course.
________________________________________
15. This is a huge system-design lesson
Infrastructure-level retry doesn't automatically mean:
"Safe to retry."
Because the network doesn't necessarily know the business semantics of the operation.
For example:
GET /users/123
is usually much easier to retry safely.
But:
POST /payments
may not be.
So the architecture eventually needs:
Load Balancer
      ↓
Timeout
      ↓
Retry?
      ↓
Idempotency?
      ↓
Business semantics
This is why senior system design isn't about memorizing components.
It's about understanding how failure propagates between components.
