Part A — What exactly is a Socket?
Don't think of a socket as something mysterious.
Think of it as:
An operating-system object representing one endpoint of network communication.
Your Java code sees something like:
Java
  ↓
Socket
  ↓
Operating System
  ↓
TCP
  ↓
Network
A socket is not the network connection itself in the conceptual sense; it's the OS interface/handle through which a process interacts with that network endpoint.
________________________________________
1. Think about a file
In Linux, many things are represented through file descriptors.
For example:
File
 ↓
file descriptor
 ↓
read()
write()
Network sockets work similarly:
Socket
 ↓
file descriptor
 ↓
read()
write()
For example, conceptually:
int fd = socket(...);
The OS gives the process a descriptor:
fd = 57
Then:
read(57, ...)
write(57, ...)
can interact with the socket.
So when we say:
"Socket 57 is ready to read"
we mean:
"The OS knows that network data associated with socket 57 can now be consumed without blocking."
________________________________________
2. Why do we need a socket?
Imagine your Java server is listening on:
10.0.0.10:8080
The operating system needs some way to represent:
"I want to receive TCP connections arriving at port 8080."
Your server creates a listening socket.
Conceptually:
Java application
      │
      │ socket()
      ▼
Linux
      │
      ▼
Listening socket
      │
      ▼
10.0.0.10:8080
Then:
bind()
listen()
accept()
are involved.
________________________________________
3. Listening socket vs connected socket
This is extremely important.
Suppose your Spring Boot application listens on:
0.0.0.0:8080
There is a listening socket.
              Server
                 │
                 ▼
       Listening Socket
             :8080
Now three clients connect:
Client A ──────────────┐
Client B ──────────────┼──► Server :8080
Client C ──────────────┘
The OS doesn't have one socket representing all three conversations.
Conceptually, you now get:
Listening Socket
       │
       ├──► Connection Socket A
       │
       ├──► Connection Socket B
       │
       └──► Connection Socket C
This is one of the most important things to understand.
________________________________________
4. Why do we need separate sockets?
Because the server needs to distinguish:
Client A
from:
Client B
Suppose:
Client A:
49.205.10.25:50001
and:
Client B:
49.205.10.26:50002
both connect to:
Server:
10.0.0.10:8080
The OS can identify their TCP flows using the connection tuple:
Client A:

49.205.10.25:50001
        ↓
10.0.0.10:8080
and:
Client B:

49.205.10.26:50002
        ↓
10.0.0.10:8080
So conceptually:
                 Server
                   │
             :8080 listening
                   │
        ┌──────────┴──────────┐
        ▼                     ▼
   Socket A              Socket B
        │                     │
   Client A               Client B
________________________________________
5. Now your question: "Request waits for socket"
My previous wording was too loose.
A request doesn't wait for a socket.
A better statement is:
A thread/event loop waits for a socket to become ready for I/O.
For example:
Client
   │
   │ sends TCP data
   ▼
Network
   │
   ▼
Server NIC
   │
   ▼
Linux kernel
   │
   ▼
Socket receive buffer
The data now exists inside the OS.
The Java application hasn't necessarily read it yet.
The OS can tell the event loop:
"Socket #57 has data available."
Then:
Event Loop
     │
     ▼
Socket #57 is readable
     │
     ▼
read()
     │
     ▼
data
That's what I meant.
________________________________________
6. What does "socket is readable" mean?
This is another important distinction.
It does not necessarily mean:
"A complete HTTP request is available."
It can mean:
A read operation can make progress without blocking.
For example, the network might deliver:
GET /orders
in pieces.
First packet:
GET /ord
Second packet:
ers/123 HTTP/1.1
The TCP layer reassembles the byte stream.
The application ultimately sees a stream of bytes.
So:
Socket
   ↓
bytes
   ↓
HTTP parser
   ↓
HTTP request
That's a very important layer separation.
________________________________________
Part B — Connection vs HTTP Request
Now let's fix the second concept.
This is probably the more important one.
________________________________________
1. Connection
A TCP connection is a communication channel between two endpoints.
For example:
Client                         Server
49.205.10.25:50001 ─────────► 10.0.0.10:8080
Once TCP handshake completes:
Client ═══════════════════════ Server
             TCP connection
The connection can remain open.
________________________________________
2. HTTP request
An HTTP request is data sent over that connection.
For example:
GET /users/101 HTTP/1.1
Host: example.com
So think:
TCP connection
      │
      │ carries bytes
      ▼
HTTP request
The connection is the communication channel.
The HTTP request is a message/data transmitted through that channel.
________________________________________
3. One connection can carry one request
The simplest case:
Client
   │
   │ TCP connection
   ▼
Server

GET /users/1
Then connection closes.
Conceptually:
Connection
└── HTTP Request #1
________________________________________
4. But one connection can carry multiple requests
This is where it gets interesting.
With HTTP keep-alive:
Client
   │
   │ TCP connection
   ▼
Server
The client can send:
Request 1:
GET /users/1

Request 2:
GET /users/2

Request 3:
GET /users/3
So:
             ONE TCP CONNECTION
                     │
        ┌────────────┼────────────┐
        ▼            ▼            ▼
     Request 1    Request 2    Request 3
That's the key.
Connection ≠ Request
A connection is the pipe.
Requests are messages travelling through the pipe.
________________________________________
5. Real-world analogy
Imagine a railway track.
Railway track
     │
     ├── Train 1
     ├── Train 2
     └── Train 3
The railway track is analogous to:
TCP connection
The trains are analogous to:
HTTP requests
You don't build a new railway track every time a new train travels.
Similarly:
TCP connection
can carry:
HTTP request 1
HTTP request 2
HTTP request 3
...
________________________________________
6. Why this matters to Load Balancing
Suppose:
Client
   │
   │ ONE TCP connection
   ▼
Load Balancer
   │
   ▼
Backend A
The client sends:
GET /users/1
GET /users/2
GET /users/3
The LB may have associated that connection with:
Backend A
So conceptually:
TCP Connection #123
        │
        ├── HTTP Request 1 ──► A
        ├── HTTP Request 2 ──► A
        └── HTTP Request 3 ──► A
This is why I said:
Load balancing a connection is not necessarily the same as load balancing individual HTTP requests.
Later, when we study HTTP/2, this gets even more interesting.
