Lesson 6 — How does a Load Balancer know a server is alive?
Suppose we have:
                    Load Balancer
                  /       |       \
                 /        |        \
                ▼         ▼         ▼
              A ✓       B ✓       C ✓
Everything is fine.
Now:
Backend B
    ↓
CRASH
Reality:
                    Load Balancer
                  /       |       \
                 ▼        ▼        ▼
                A        B 💀      C
But the LB doesn't magically know B died.
It needs failure detection.
And this is much harder than it sounds.
________________________________________
1. The first naive idea
The LB could simply send traffic to B:
LB
 |
 | GET /orders/123
 ▼
B
If B doesn't respond:
timeout
LB concludes:
B might be dead
This is called passive failure detection.
The LB learns about health while handling real traffic.
________________________________________
2. Passive health checking
Imagine:
Request 1 → B → timeout
Request 2 → B → timeout
Request 3 → B → timeout
After several failures:
B = unhealthy
So:
Before:

A ✓
B ✓
C ✓


After:

A ✓
B ✗
C ✓
The LB stops sending traffic to B.
Simple.
But there is a problem.
________________________________________
3. Real users discover the failure first
Suppose B has just crashed.
The first user request:
User
  ↓
LB
  ↓
B 💀
The user experiences:
timeout
Only after that does the LB learn:
B is dead.
That's undesirable.
We would rather detect failure before users encounter it.
So we introduce:
Active Health Checks
________________________________________
4. Active health check
The LB periodically asks:
"Are you alive?"
For example:
LB ───────► B
           GET /health
B responds:
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
LB:
B = healthy ✓
Then after some interval:
LB ───────► B
           GET /health
Again:
200 OK
So:
B = healthy
________________________________________
5. What happens when B crashes?
Before:
LB ───────► B
           /health
             ↓
           200 OK
After crash:
LB ───────► B 💀
           /health
             ↓
          timeout
LB marks:
B = unhealthy
and removes B from the backend selection pool.
Now:
                    LB
                  /    \
                 ▼      ▼
                A       C
No new requests go to B.
________________________________________
6. But there's a subtle problem
What does this mean?
GET /health → 200 OK
Does it mean:
"This server can successfully process production traffic?"
Not necessarily.
This distinction is extremely important.
________________________________________
7. Liveness vs Readiness
Consider a Java Spring Boot application.
The JVM is alive:
JVM ✓
But:
Database 💀
Redis 💀
Kafka 💀
Thread pool exhausted
The process is alive.
But can it actually serve requests?
Maybe not.
So we separate:
Liveness
from:
Readiness
________________________________________
8. Liveness
Liveness asks:
Is the application process alive enough that restarting it might help?
Conceptually:
GET /live
Response:
200 OK
means:
Application process appears alive.
If liveness fails repeatedly, an orchestrator such as Kubernetes may restart the application.
________________________________________
9. Readiness
Readiness asks:
Should this instance receive traffic right now?
This is different.
Suppose:
JVM = alive ✓
Database connection = unavailable ✗
The process is alive.
But maybe it shouldn't receive traffic.
So:
/live
→ 200 OK

/ready
→ 503 Service Unavailable
Then:
Process = alive
Traffic eligibility = NO
That's a powerful distinction.
________________________________________
10. Think about deployment
Imagine we're deploying version 2.
We start:
Server A → version 1
Server B → version 1
Server C → version 1
Then launch:
Server D → version 2
D starts:
JVM starting...
Spring Boot initializing...
DB pool initializing...
Caches warming...
Kafka consumers starting...
If the LB immediately sends traffic:
LB → D
we could send production requests to a server that isn't ready.
So D should initially be:
D = alive
D = NOT READY
Only after initialization:
D = READY
Then:
LB → D
This is why readiness is crucial during deployments.
________________________________________
11. Now let's go deeper
Suppose our health check runs every:
5 seconds
And B crashes immediately after a successful check.
Timeline:
12:00:00 → health check ✓
12:00:01 → B crashes
12:00:02 → B dead
12:00:03 → B dead
12:00:04 → B dead
12:00:05 → health check ✗
The LB could potentially continue considering B healthy for several seconds.
This gives us an important concept:
Failure detection is not instantaneous.
There is always some detection delay.
________________________________________
12. Health-check interval
Suppose:
interval = 5 sec
The LB checks:
0s
5s
10s
15s
...
Smaller interval:
1 sec
means faster detection.
But now the LB creates more health-check traffic.
Suppose:
10,000 servers
At:
1 health check/sec
that's:
10,000 health requests/sec
just for monitoring.
So:
faster detection
        ↕
more monitoring overhead
This is a trade-off.
________________________________________
13. But one failed health check shouldn't necessarily kill a server
Suppose:
Health check #1 → timeout
Was B really dead?
Maybe:
network packet lost
temporary GC pause
CPU spike
LB overloaded
network congestion
If the LB immediately removes B:
B ✗
it may create unnecessary traffic redistribution.
So production systems commonly use failure thresholds.
For example:
3 consecutive failures → unhealthy
________________________________________
14. Example
Suppose:
threshold = 3
Health checks:
Check 1 → failure
Check 2 → success
Check 3 → failure
Check 4 → failure
Check 5 → failure
Only after three consecutive failures:
B = unhealthy
So:
F
S
F
F
F
The final three failures trigger removal.
________________________________________
15. Recovery is also dangerous
Suppose B crashed.
The LB removes it:
A ✓
B ✗
C ✓
B restarts.
Immediately:
B → 200 OK
Should the LB instantly send:
100% traffic → B
?
No.
Imagine B is still warming up.
Maybe:
JVM started
but
cache = cold
DB pool = warming
JIT = warming
connections = not established
So recovery needs a success threshold too.
For example:
3 consecutive successful checks → healthy
________________________________________
16. Now we have two state transitions
Failure:
Healthy
   ↓
failure
   ↓
failure
   ↓
failure
   ↓
Unhealthy
Recovery:
Unhealthy
   ↓
success
   ↓
success
   ↓
success
   ↓
Healthy
This prevents reacting to every tiny network fluctuation.
________________________________________
17. The server that keeps changing state
Now we encounter a nasty case.
Suppose:
B
is unstable.
Health checks:
✓
✗
✓
✗
✓
✗
The LB keeps doing:
Healthy
   ↓
Unhealthy
   ↓
Healthy
   ↓
Unhealthy
   ↓
...
This is called flapping.
It can create instability:
traffic → B
traffic removed
traffic → B
traffic removed
...
And every time B is removed, its traffic gets pushed onto A and C.
So one unstable server can indirectly overload healthy servers.
________________________________________
18. This is a feedback loop
Imagine:
B becomes slow
      ↓
LB removes B
      ↓
A + C receive more traffic
      ↓
A + C become overloaded
      ↓
A + C become slow
      ↓
LB removes A/C
      ↓
traffic shifts again
This is no longer merely:
"Is B alive?"
It's a system stability problem.
That's the kind of thing senior engineers need to recognize.
________________________________________
19. Health check timeout
Suppose:
LB → B
GET /health
What if B never responds?
The LB cannot wait forever.
It needs:
healthCheckTimeout = 1 second
So:
send request
     ↓
wait
     ↓
1 sec
     ↓
no response
     ↓
FAIL
Otherwise one dead backend can cause the health-check thread to block indefinitely.
________________________________________
20. Important distinction: timeout ≠ server crash
Suppose:
health check timeout
We only know:
The LB did not receive a valid response within the configured time.
We don't know exactly why.
Possibilities:
B crashed
B is overloaded
network packet lost
network partition
B has a long GC pause
LB cannot reach B
B's health endpoint is blocked
This is a fundamental distributed-systems concept:
Failure is often observed indirectly.
The LB cannot see "B is dead."
It observes:
"I can't successfully communicate with B."
That's different.
________________________________________
21. Network partition
This leads to one of the most important concepts we'll later revisit in CAP.
Suppose:
           Network
        XXXXXXXXXXXXX
B is actually healthy:
B ✓
But the LB cannot reach it:
LB ───────X─────── B
The LB concludes:
B = unavailable
From LB's perspective:
B is unreachable
From B's perspective:
"I'm perfectly healthy!"
Both can be correct.
This is why distributed systems cannot simply ask:
"Is the server alive?"
They have to reason about reachability and observability.
________________________________________
22. Active vs passive health checks
Let's compare.
	Active	Passive
How?	Dedicated health request	Observe real requests
Detect before user traffic?	Usually yes	No
Extra traffic?	Yes	No
Can detect endpoint-specific problems?	Depends on check	Yes
Can detect network failure?	Yes	Yes
Can observe real workload?	Limited	Yes
A robust production architecture can use both.
________________________________________
23. A powerful combination
Imagine:
                    Load Balancer
                         |
              ┌──────────┴──────────┐
              │                     │
        Active checks          Real traffic
              │                     │
              ▼                     ▼
        /ready endpoint       request failures
              │                     │
              └──────────┬──────────┘
                         ▼
                    Health state
So:
Active check says:
"Are you ready?"

Passive observations say:
"Are real requests actually succeeding?"
Much richer information.
________________________________________
24. But now we have a subtle issue
Suppose B is receiving:
1000 requests/sec
and suddenly we decide:
B = unhealthy
What happens to existing connections?
Suppose:
Client
   │
   │ TCP connection
   ▼
LB
   │
   ▼
B
There might already be:
100 active requests
If the LB simply kills B's connections:
💥
users may see:
connection reset
500
timeout
partial response
So we need another concept.
Connection Draining
________________________________________
25. Connection draining
Suppose B is being removed.
Instead of:
B → DEAD immediately
we say:
B → DRAINING
Meaning:
Don't send new traffic to B, but allow existing work to finish.
State:
Healthy
   ↓
Draining
   ↓
Removed
During draining:
New request ──X──► B
but:
Existing request ─────► B
                      finish
This is extremely useful during:
deployments
autoscaling
maintenance
instance termination
rolling updates
________________________________________
26. Example
Suppose:
B has 50 active requests
We want to terminate B.
Without draining:
kill B
 ↓
50 requests potentially fail
With draining:
stop new requests
 ↓
50 → 40 → 20 → 5 → 0
 ↓
terminate B
Much safer.
________________________________________
27. But what if a request takes 30 minutes?
Now draining can become dangerous.
Imagine:
B
 └── request running for 30 minutes
If we wait forever:
B never terminates
So draining needs a maximum drain timeout.
Example:
Drain timeout = 30 sec
After 30 seconds:
remaining requests
       ↓
force termination
This creates a trade-off:
long drain
    ↓
fewer failed requests
but
slower deployments
versus:
short drain
    ↓
faster deployment
but
more interrupted requests
Again:
There is rarely a perfect setting.
________________________________________
28. Graceful shutdown in Java
This becomes very relevant to your Java background.
Suppose your Spring Boot application receives:
SIGTERM
during deployment.
A production-friendly sequence is:
SIGTERM
   ↓
Application enters shutdown
   ↓
Readiness becomes false
   ↓
LB stops sending NEW traffic
   ↓
Existing requests finish
   ↓
Connection draining
   ↓
Application terminates
Notice the ordering.
You don't want:
kill JVM
   ↓
LB discovers dead server
because users may receive failures.
Instead:
remove from traffic
   ↓
finish existing work
   ↓
shutdown
This is one of the most important deployment patterns to understand.
________________________________________
29. Let's put the entire lifecycle together
A backend might move through:
                 ┌───────────────┐
                 │   STARTING    │
                 └───────┬───────┘
                         │
                   readiness OK
                         │
                         ▼
                 ┌───────────────┐
                 │    HEALTHY    │
                 └───────┬───────┘
                         │
                  failure threshold
                         │
                         ▼
                 ┌───────────────┐
                 │   UNHEALTHY   │
                 └───────┬───────┘
                         │
                 recovery checks
                         │
                         ▼
                 ┌───────────────┐
                 │    HEALTHY    │
                 └───────────────┘
And during deployment:
HEALTHY
   │
   │ shutdown requested
   ▼
DRAINING
   │
   │ active requests finish
   ▼
REMOVED
This is a much more realistic model than:
UP / DOWN
________________________________________
30. Now let's build a simplified health checker
Conceptually:
class HealthChecker {

    void check(Backend backend) {

        try {
            Response response =
                httpGet(
                    backend.url() + "/ready",
                    Duration.ofSeconds(1)
                );

            if (response.statusCode() == 200) {
                backend.recordSuccess();
            } else {
                backend.recordFailure();
            }

        } catch (TimeoutException e) {
            backend.recordFailure();

        } catch (IOException e) {
            backend.recordFailure();
        }
    }
}
And backend state:
class Backend {

    int consecutiveFailures;
    int consecutiveSuccesses;

    boolean healthy;

    void recordFailure() {
        consecutiveFailures++;
        consecutiveSuccesses = 0;

        if (consecutiveFailures >= 3) {
            healthy = false;
        }
    }

    void recordSuccess() {
        consecutiveSuccesses++;
        consecutiveFailures = 0;

        if (consecutiveSuccesses >= 3) {
            healthy = true;
        }
    }
}
This is simplified, but now you can see that a health check isn't merely:
if (200) healthy
else unhealthy
There is state and history.
________________________________________
31. One thing I'd change in production
Don't make the health checker itself a single blocking thread:
check A
wait
check B
wait
check C
wait
...
If one server takes:
1 second
you can delay checks for everyone.
Instead, health checks should be scheduled/concurrent, with carefully controlled resource usage.
And at very large scale, you have to think about:
threads
connections
timers
event loops
timeouts
DNS
TLS handshakes
metrics
The monitoring system itself can become a bottleneck.
________________________________________
32. The deep lesson
A load balancer isn't simply:
Client → LB → Server
It's actually maintaining a belief about backend state:
LB's belief:

A → probably healthy
B → probably unhealthy
C → probably healthy
Notice the word:
probably
The LB never has perfect knowledge.
It makes decisions based on observations.
That is a fundamental distributed-systems idea.
________________________________________
