Load Balancer — Lesson 7
Architecture Internals: NIC → Socket → Kernel → Event Loop → Backend
Our goal is to understand this path:
Client
   │
   │ Ethernet / IP / TCP
   ▼
┌───────────────────────┐
│   Load Balancer Host  │
│                       │
│  NIC                  │
│   ↓                   │
│  Kernel               │
│   ↓                   │
│  Socket               │
│   ↓                   │
│  epoll                │
│   ↓                   │
│  Event Loop           │
│   ↓                   │
│  HTTP Parser          │
│   ↓                   │
│  LB Algorithm         │
│   ↓                   │
│  Backend Connection   │
└───────────┬───────────┘
            │
            ▼
        Backend
We're going to dissect every layer.
________________________________________
1. Start with the physical reality
Suppose your browser wants:
GET /orders/123
It doesn't send a Java String directly to the server.
Eventually, the request becomes bytes.
Conceptually:
HTTP
 ↓
TCP
 ↓
IP
 ↓
Ethernet
 ↓
Network
Something like:
+-------------------+
| Ethernet Header   |
+-------------------+
| IP Header         |
+-------------------+
| TCP Header        |
+-------------------+
| HTTP bytes        |
+-------------------+
The network carries bits.
The operating system turns those incoming bits into structures your application can read.
________________________________________
2. The NIC
The first major component on the load-balancer machine is the:
Network Interface Card (NIC).
Think:
Internet
    │
    │ electrical / optical / radio signals
    ▼
   NIC
The NIC receives packets.
But your Java application isn't directly reading electrical signals.
The NIC and operating system cooperate to move packets into memory.
A simplified path:
Network
   ↓
NIC
   ↓
DMA
   ↓
RAM
   ↓
Linux kernel networking stack
   ↓
Socket receive buffer
The important term here is:
DMA — Direct Memory Access
The NIC can transfer packet data into memory without the CPU manually copying every byte from the hardware.
This dramatically reduces CPU overhead.
________________________________________
3. The kernel gets involved
Once the packet enters the machine:
NIC
 ↓
Kernel networking stack
Linux processes layers roughly like:
Ethernet
   ↓
IP
   ↓
TCP
   ↓
Socket
The kernel looks at the packet.
For example:
Destination IP:
10.0.0.10

Destination port:
443
It needs to determine:
Which socket should receive this data?
This brings us to the word you previously asked about.
Socket
________________________________________
4. What exactly is a socket?
A socket is not the network cable.
A socket is an OS-managed communication endpoint.
Think of it as:
Application
     │
     │ read/write
     ▼
┌─────────────┐
│   Socket    │
└─────────────┘
     │
     ▼
Kernel networking
     │
     ▼
Network
Your Java application interacts with the socket.
The kernel handles the complicated networking underneath.
________________________________________
5. Server socket vs connected socket
This is one of the most important distinctions.
Suppose our load balancer listens on:
0.0.0.0:443
It creates a listening socket.
Conceptually:
socket()
   ↓
bind()
   ↓
listen()
Then:
┌─────────────────────────┐
│ Listening Socket        │
│ 0.0.0.0:443             │
└─────────────────────────┘
It means:
"I'm willing to accept incoming TCP connections on port 443."
It isn't yet a connection to one specific client.
________________________________________
6. Client connects
Suppose:
Client:
192.168.1.20:53124

LB:
10.0.0.10:443
The client establishes TCP.
Conceptually:
Client
192.168.1.20:53124
       │
       │ TCP
       ▼
10.0.0.10:443
Load Balancer
TCP handshake:
Client                    LB

   SYN ──────────────────►

       ◄──────────────── SYN + ACK

   ACK ──────────────────►
Now a TCP connection exists.
________________________________________
7. The listening socket isn't the actual conversation
This is critical.
The listening socket:
0.0.0.0:443
is used to accept connections.
After the connection is accepted, the kernel creates/returns a connected socket associated with that particular client.
Conceptually:
Listening Socket
      │
      │ accept()
      ▼
Connected Socket
      │
      ├── Client IP
      ├── Client port
      ├── Server IP
      ├── Server port
      └── TCP state
So:
Listening socket
=
"Give me new connections."

Connected socket
=
"Talk to this particular client."
This distinction is fundamental.
________________________________________
8. Four pieces identify a TCP connection
A TCP connection is associated with:
Source IP
Source Port
Destination IP
Destination Port
For example:
192.168.1.20 : 53124
        ↓
10.0.0.10 : 443
Another client:
192.168.1.21 : 53125
        ↓
10.0.0.10 : 443
Both connect to the same server port:
443
but they're different connections.
________________________________________
9. What does accept() actually do?
Consider simplified C-like server code:
int server_fd = socket(...);

bind(server_fd, ...);

listen(server_fd, ...);

while (1) {
    int client_fd = accept(server_fd, ...);

    // communicate with client_fd
}
Important:
server_fd
is the listening socket.
And:
client_fd
represents the newly accepted connection.
So:
listen socket
      │
      │ accept()
      ▼
client socket
________________________________________
10. Now here's the problem
Imagine:
1,000,000 clients
connecting to our LB.
A naive design could do:
Thread 1 → Client 1
Thread 2 → Client 2
Thread 3 → Client 3
...
Thread 1,000,000 → Client 1,000,000
Obviously terrible.
Why?
Each thread consumes memory and scheduling resources.
This leads us to an important historical problem.
C10K Problem
________________________________________
11. C10K
C10K roughly refers to the challenge of handling:
10,000 concurrent connections on one machine.
It became a famous systems problem because the traditional:
one thread per connection
model didn't scale well.
And modern systems may need:
100K
1M+
connections.
So the architecture evolved toward:
Many connections
      ↓
Few threads
      ↓
Event notification
That's the key.
________________________________________
12. Instead of asking every socket...
Imagine we have:
Socket 1
Socket 2
Socket 3
...
Socket 100,000
Naive application:
check socket 1
check socket 2
check socket 3
...
check socket 100000
Most sockets might have nothing to read.
That's wasteful.
We want the OS to tell us:
"These 17 sockets are ready."
That's the basic idea behind:
epoll
________________________________________
13. epoll mental model
Think of epoll as:
                    Linux Kernel
                         │
             ┌───────────┴───────────┐
             │                       │
         Socket A                 Socket B
         no data                  data ready
             │                       │
             └───────────┬───────────┘
                         │
                       epoll
                         │
                         ▼
                  "B is ready"
The application doesn't need to continuously inspect every socket.
It asks the kernel:
"Tell me which registered sockets
are ready for I/O."
________________________________________
14. Event loop
Now we can create a single event-loop thread:
while (running) {

    events = epoll_wait();

    for (event : events) {

        if (event.isReadable()) {
            handleRead(event.socket);
        }

        if (event.isWritable()) {
            handleWrite(event.socket);
        }
    }
}
Conceptually:
                 Event Loop
                     │
              epoll_wait()
                     │
           ┌─────────┼─────────┐
           ▼         ▼         ▼
         Sock A    Sock C    Sock F
         ready     ready     ready
One thread can manage a huge number of sockets.
________________________________________
15. This is the key architectural shift
Old model:
Connection
     ↓
Thread
Modern high-performance model:
Connections
     ↓
Event loop
     ↓
Few threads
Instead of:
100,000 connections
100,000 threads
you might have:
100,000 connections
       ↓
8 event-loop threads
The exact architecture varies, but the principle is huge.
________________________________________
16. What does "ready" mean?
Suppose:
Client → LB
and TCP data arrives.
The kernel puts data into a receive buffer.
Now the socket becomes readable.
The event loop gets:
READABLE
Then:
read(socket, buffer)
copies/returns available data to the application.
Similarly, if the socket can accept more outgoing data, it can become writable.
So event-driven I/O revolves around:
READABLE
WRITABLE
and other events.
________________________________________
17. Important: epoll doesn't read the data
This is a common misunderstanding.
epoll does not mean:
"Here is the HTTP request."
It means more like:
"This socket currently has I/O work that your application may be able to perform."
Then your application calls:
read()
or:
recv()
to actually retrieve bytes.
So:
epoll
  ↓
"socket 17 is ready"
  ↓
read(socket17)
  ↓
bytes
________________________________________
18. Where does HTTP enter?
Suppose the bytes received are:
GET /orders/123 HTTP/1.1\r\n
Host: example.com\r\n
Connection: keep-alive\r\n
\r\n
The kernel doesn't understand:
GET
/orders/123
Host
That's application-level protocol data.
The Java load balancer has to parse it.
So:
TCP bytes
    ↓
HTTP parser
    ↓
HTTP request
This gives us another important distinction:
TCP
=
byte stream
while:
HTTP
=
application protocol encoded into those bytes
________________________________________
19. TCP doesn't preserve HTTP request boundaries
This is extremely important.
Suppose the client sends:
Request A
Request B
TCP might deliver bytes to your application like:
READ #1:
"GET /ord"

READ #2:
"ers/123 HTTP/1.1\r\n..."

READ #3:
"GET /users/5 HTTP/1.1..."
Or potentially multiple requests in one read.
So the application cannot assume:
1 read() = 1 HTTP request
This is a critical networking concept.
________________________________________
20. Why?
Because TCP is a:
reliable ordered byte stream
not:
message-oriented protocol.
Therefore your HTTP parser must reconstruct protocol messages from a stream of bytes.
Conceptually:
TCP stream
──────────────────────────────►

GET /orders/123 HTTP/1.1\r\n...
              ↑
        HTTP parser
              ↓
        complete request
________________________________________
21. Now the Load Balancer has work to do
Once the LB parses:
GET /orders/123
it needs to determine:
Which backend?
So:
Client
   ↓
TCP bytes
   ↓
socket
   ↓
epoll
   ↓
read()
   ↓
HTTP parser
   ↓
HTTP Request
   ↓
Load balancing algorithm
   ↓
Backend B
This is the path we wanted to understand.
________________________________________
22. But the LB now needs another socket
Remember:
Client
    ↓
LB
The LB also needs:
LB
    ↓
Backend
Therefore the LB is sitting between two network connections.
Conceptually:
Client
   │
   │ TCP connection #1
   ▼
┌───────────────┐
│ Load Balancer │
└───────────────┘
   │
   │ TCP connection #2
   ▼
Backend
This is a critical mental model.
The LB isn't magically forwarding a TCP packet from one machine to another in the basic reverse-proxy model.
It may terminate the client connection and create a separate upstream connection.
________________________________________
23. Connection mapping
Imagine:
Client:
10.1.1.5:51000

LB:
10.1.1.10:443

Backend:
10.1.2.20:8080
There can be:
Client ───── TCP #1 ───── LB

LB ───────── TCP #2 ───── Backend
The LB acts as an intermediary.
The HTTP request is:
read from client
       ↓
parse
       ↓
select backend
