19. Now meet TIME_WAIT
This is an important production issue.
After closing certain TCP connections, one side can enter:
TIME_WAIT
Why?
Among other reasons, TCP needs to ensure that delayed packets from an old connection don't get confused with a future connection using the same connection tuple.
It's a safety mechanism.
________________________________________
20. Why should you care as a Java developer?
Imagine your application creates huge numbers of outbound connections:
Java Service
   ↓
Database
or:
Java Service
   ↓
Redis
or:
Java Service
   ↓
Another API
If you continuously create and close connections instead of reusing them:
connect
close
connect
close
connect
close
...
you can create:
TIME_WAIT
TIME_WAIT
TIME_WAIT
TIME_WAIT
...
and eventually encounter resource/port pressure.
This is one reason connection pooling is so important.
________________________________________
21. Connection pooling
Instead of:
Request 1 → create TCP connection → close
Request 2 → create TCP connection → close
Request 3 → create TCP connection → close
use:
                Connection Pool
             ┌──────┬──────┬──────┐
             │ TCP1 │ TCP2 │ TCP3 │
             └──┬───┴──┬───┴──┬───┘
                │       │       │
              reuse   reuse   reuse
Application requests borrow existing connections.
This reduces:
TCP handshakes
TLS handshakes
CPU
latency
TIME_WAIT churn
This concept will become very important when we discuss:
Load Balancer
Redis
Databases
Microservices
HTTP clients
