27. A real-world latency thought experiment
Suppose:
TCP RTT = 50ms
TLS setup = additional network/CPU cost
HTTP request = 10ms server processing
A fresh connection for every request means you repeatedly pay connection-establishment costs.
But with a persistent connection:
Connect once
     ↓
Request 1
Request 2
Request 3
Request 4
...
the connection setup cost is amortized across many requests.
This is why connection reuse is one of the simplest and most powerful performance optimizations in distributed systems.
________________________________________
28. But don't blindly make pools huge
A common beginner mistake:
"More connections = more performance."
No.
Suppose backend supports:
1000 concurrent requests
and your LB creates:
100,000 connections
You may create:
memory pressure
context switching
TLS state
kernel overhead
connection contention
backend overload
The optimal connection count depends on:
request concurrency
latency
CPU
backend capacity
network bandwidth
protocol
connection lifetime
This is a capacity planning problem.
________________________________________
29. Senior-level mental model
Don't think:
"HTTP Keep-Alive means connection stays open."
Think:
Connection establishment
        ↓
expensive
        ↓
reuse connection
        ↓
amortize handshake cost
        ↓
reduce latency
        ↓
reduce CPU
        ↓
reduce connection churn
        ↓
reduce TIME_WAIT / socket pressure
Then:
HTTP/1.1
    ↓
persistent connections
    ↓
multiple connections for concurrency
Then:
HTTP/2
    ↓
multiplexing
    ↓
many streams over fewer TCP connections
Then:
HTTP/3
    ↓
QUIC
    ↓
independent transport streams
That's the evolution.
________________________________________
30. One important correction to remember
Don't say:
"HTTP/2 uses one TCP connection."
That's not necessarily true.
HTTP/2 can multiplex many streams over a TCP connection, but implementations can use more than one connection depending on circumstances.
Likewise:
"HTTP/3 uses UDP directly."
More accurately:
HTTP/3
  ↓
QUIC
  ↓
UDP
QUIC provides the transport semantics.
