├── FD 6 → client C
└── ...
________________________________________
14.6 This is why thousands of connections are possible
You can have:
FD 4
FD 5
FD 6
...
FD 10000
Each FD refers to a different kernel-managed resource.
Therefore:
10,000 connections
can correspond roughly to:
10,000 socket FDs
The OS keeps track of them.
________________________________________
14.7 Now comes epoll
We have:
FD 4 → Client A
FD 5 → Client B
FD 6 → Client C
...
FD 10000 → Client N
We want Linux to tell us:
Which of these sockets currently has something useful to do?
That's where epoll enters.
________________________________________
14.8 Creating an epoll instance
Conceptually:
epoll_create()
returns another FD.
For example:
FD 7 → epoll instance
Now the process has:
FD 3 → listening socket
FD 4 → client A
FD 5 → client B
FD 6 → client C

FD 7 → epoll instance
Notice something interesting:
Even the epoll instance itself is represented by a file descriptor.
Linux treats many resources through the same FD abstraction.
________________________________________
14.9 Registering a socket
Suppose:
FD 4 → Client A
We tell epoll:
Watch FD 4 and notify me when it becomes readable.
Conceptually:
epoll_ctl(
    epollFD,
    ADD,
    socketFD = 4,
    READ
)
Now:
              epoll FD 7
                   │
                   ▼
              watches
                   │
                   ▼
                FD 4
                   │
                   ▼
              Client A
We do the same:
FD 5 → watch
FD 6 → watch
FD 8 → watch
...
________________________________________
14.10 What does "watch" mean?
This is crucial.
The application isn't repeatedly asking:
"Is FD 4 ready?"

"Is FD 5 ready?"

"Is FD 6 ready?"
Instead it tells the kernel:
Watch these FDs for me.
The kernel then monitors the underlying resources as part of its networking machinery.
________________________________________
14.11 The kernel already receives packets
Remember:
Network
   ↓
Network interface
   ↓
Linux kernel
   ↓
TCP/IP stack
   ↓
Socket receive buffer
The proxy process isn't directly watching Ethernet packets.
The kernel handles them first.
Suppose:
Client A
   │
   │ GET /orders
   ▼
Network card
   ▼
Linux kernel
   ▼
TCP processing
   ▼
Socket FD 4
The bytes become available to the socket.
Now FD 4 is considered readable.
________________________________________
14.12 What does "readable" actually mean?
It does not necessarily mean:
"The entire HTTP request is available."
It generally means:
A read operation can make progress without blocking.
For example:
FD 4
   ↓
some bytes available
The proxy can call:
read(fd4)
and get data.
That may be:
GET /ord
rather than the complete:
GET /orders/123 HTTP/1.1
...
This is extremely important for network programming.
________________________________________
14.13 TCP is a byte stream
TCP doesn't understand:
HTTP request
TCP sees:
bytes
bytes
bytes
bytes
So the client may send:
GET /orders/123 HTTP/1.1
but the proxy might receive:
GET /ord
first.
Then:
ers/123 HT
Then:
TP/1.1...
The HTTP parser must accumulate bytes until it has enough information to parse the HTTP message.
Therefore:
epoll readable
doesn't mean:
HTTP request complete
It means:
socket has data that can currently be read
________________________________________
14.14 The event loop calls epoll_wait
Now the proxy has registered:
FD 4
FD 5
FD 6
...
FD 10000
The event loop calls:
epoll_wait()
Conceptually:
Application
     │
     │ epoll_wait()
     ▼
Linux Kernel
     │
     │
     │ waits...
     │
The event-loop thread can sleep.
It isn't burning CPU scanning thousands of sockets.
________________________________________
14.15 Then a packet arrives
Suppose Client B sends:
GET /payments
Network path:
Client B
   ↓
Network interface
   ↓
Linux kernel
   ↓
TCP/IP stack
   ↓
Socket FD 5
The socket now has data.
The kernel knows:
FD 5 is readable
And because FD 5 is registered with epoll:
epoll
   ↓
FD 5 becomes ready
________________________________________
14.16 epoll_wait() wakes up
The sleeping event loop gets notified.
Conceptually:
epoll_wait()
     │
     │ sleeping
     │
     ▼
Kernel detects FD 5 ready
     │
     ▼
wake event-loop thread
     │
     ▼
epoll_wait() returns
The returned event tells the application:
FD 5 → readable
Now the event loop knows exactly what to process.
________________________________________
14.17 This is the scalability trick
Imagine:
100,000 connections
Only:
17 connections
have activity right now.
The event loop gets something conceptually like:
FD 512 → readable
FD 781 → readable
FD 1002 → writable
FD 2341 → readable
...
It doesn't need to inspect all 100,000 connections one by one.
It processes the ready ones.
________________________________________
14.18 Where is the list of watched FDs?
At a conceptual level, think of an epoll instance as maintaining two important ideas:
                epoll instance
                     │
          ┌──────────┴──────────┐
          │                     │
          ▼                     ▼
     Interest list          Ready list
Interest list
What should I watch?
FD 4
FD 5
FD 6
FD 7
...
Ready list
Which watched FDs are currently ready?
FD 5
FD 918
FD 1203
This is a simplified mental model, not a promise about every internal kernel data structure.
But it is an excellent way to understand the mechanism.
________________________________________
14.19 Why this is better than scanning
Naive approach:
for fd in all_connections:
    check(fd)
If:
N = 100,000
you inspect:
100,000
items even if only 10 are active.
With readiness notification:
100,000 registered
       ↓
10 ready
       ↓
process approximately those 10
That's the fundamental performance advantage.
________________________________________
14.20 But does epoll magically make everything O(1)?
No.
Be careful here.
People sometimes say:
"epoll is O(1)."
That's an oversimplification.
The real performance characteristics depend on:
number of registered FDs
number of ready events
event registration/modification
kernel implementation
application processing
network workload
The important practical idea is:
You avoid repeatedly scanning every connection just to find out which ones are active.
That's the architectural advantage.
________________________________________
14.21 Now the interesting part: Level Triggered
There are two major ways to interpret readiness:
Level-triggered
Edge-triggered
Start with level-triggered.
Suppose FD 4 has:
100 bytes
available.
The socket is:
READABLE
The event loop gets:
FD 4 → readable
But suppose the application reads only:
20 bytes
There are still:
80 bytes
remaining.
The socket is still readable.
Therefore the kernel can report it again.
Conceptually:
100 bytes available
      ↓
event
      ↓
read 20
      ↓
80 bytes remain
      ↓
still readable
      ↓
event again
This is level-triggered behavior.
________________________________________
14.22 Think of a water tank
Imagine:
Tank
██████████
The rule is:
"Notify me whenever the tank contains water."
You remove some:
Tank
██████
There is still water.
So:
"Notify me again."
That's similar to level-triggered readiness.
The condition remains true.
________________________________________
14.23 Edge-triggered
Now imagine a different rule:
"Notify me when the state changes from not-ready to ready."
Initially:
0 bytes
Then data arrives:
0 → 100 bytes
That's a transition.
The kernel reports:
FD 4 → READY
Application reads only:
20 bytes
Now:
80 bytes remain
But the state didn't transition from:
not-ready → ready
again.
Therefore the application must be careful.
________________________________________
14.24 Why edge-triggered requires draining
With edge-triggered mode, the typical pattern is:
while (true) {
    read();
}
until the socket says:
EAGAIN
Conceptually:
FD becomes ready
      ↓
event
      ↓
read
      ↓
read
      ↓
read
      ↓
read
      ↓
EAGAIN
      ↓
stop
This means:
"I've consumed everything currently available."
________________________________________
14.25 What is EAGAIN?
It basically tells the non-blocking application:
There is currently nothing more to read without waiting.
So:
read()
   ↓
data
repeat.
Eventually:
read()
   ↓
EAGAIN
Then:
stop reading for now
and wait for another readiness event.
________________________________________
14.26 Why can edge-triggered code go wrong?
Suppose:
100 bytes arrive
Event:
FD 4 → ready
Application reads only:
20 bytes
80 remain.
If it assumes:
"I'll get another event."
it may not.
Result:
80 bytes
   ↓
still sitting in socket buffer
   ↓
application doesn't read them
You can effectively stall that connection.
That's why edge-triggered event loops require careful programming.
________________________________________
14.27 Another failure: 100% CPU
Now imagine the opposite.
Suppose the application keeps asking to write to a socket that is always writable.
If the event loop continuously receives:
WRITABLE
WRITABLE
WRITABLE
WRITABLE
...
and does unnecessary work, it can spin.
This is one reason event registration needs to be managed carefully.
You don't necessarily want:
WATCH WRITE
all the time.
________________________________________
14.28 Why write readiness is different
Reading:
READABLE
usually means:
there is data available to read
Writing:
WRITABLE
usually means:
the socket's send buffer has space for data
But most TCP sockets are writable most of the time.
Therefore, an event-driven server often watches for write readiness only when it actually has pending output that couldn't be written immediately.
Conceptually:
No pending response
       ↓
don't care about WRITE

Pending response
       ↓
try write()

Can't write everything
       ↓
watch WRITE

Socket becomes writable
       ↓
continue write()
This is an important event-loop optimization.
________________________________________
14.29 Now let's build the proxy event loop
A simplified conceptual loop:
while (serverRunning) {

    events = epoll_wait();

    for each event {

        if (event == NEW_CONNECTION) {
            accept();
            register(clientSocket);
        }

        if (event == READABLE) {
            read();
            parse();
            process();
        }

        if (event == WRITABLE) {
            writePendingData();
        }
    }
}
This tiny loop represents a huge amount of high-performance networking architecture.
________________________________________
14.30 But there is another problem: HTTP processing isn't instantaneous
Suppose:
FD 10 → request
The proxy reads it.
Then it performs:
routing
authentication
header processing
upstream selection
Some of those operations may take time.
If the event loop spends:
500 ms
processing one connection, other connections are delayed.
So the design principle becomes:
Keep event-loop work short and non-blocking.
________________________________________
14.31 Event loop = traffic controller
Think of the event loop as an airport controller.
There may be:
10,000 planes
but only a few need immediate attention.
The controller doesn't personally fly each plane.
Instead:
Event Loop
   │
   ├── Connection A → read
   ├── Connection B → write
   ├── Connection C → accept
   └── Connection D → read
It coordinates readiness.
This is the mental model you should retain.
________________________________________
