Now we move up one abstraction level.
Our question is:
What actually happens inside a reverse proxy when a client sends one HTTP request?
We'll trace one request all the way through.
________________________________________
16.1 The architecture
Suppose we have:
Client
   │
   │ HTTP
   ▼
Reverse Proxy
   │
   │ HTTP
   ▼
Backend Server
For example:
Browser
   │
   │ GET /orders/123
   ▼
Nginx / HAProxy / Envoy
   │
   │ GET /orders/123
   ▼
Java Spring Boot
There are actually two TCP connections involved.
Connection #1

Client ←──────────────→ Proxy


Connection #2

Proxy ←──────────────→ Backend
This is one of the most important things to understand.
The proxy isn't simply "passing a packet through."
It is sitting between two independent connections.
________________________________________
16.2 Before any request arrives
The proxy has started.
It creates a listening socket:
socket()
bind()
listen()
Suppose:
Listening FD = 3
Conceptually:
Proxy Process
      │
      ▼
FD 3
      │
      ▼
Listening TCP socket
      │
      ▼
0.0.0.0:8080
The proxy registers FD 3 with epoll:
epoll
  │
  └── FD 3 → READABLE
But here "readable" means:
A new connection can be accepted.
Not HTTP data.
That's an important distinction.
________________________________________
16.3 Client wants /orders/123
Client:
Browser
wants:
GET /orders/123
But HTTP doesn't immediately jump into the application.
First, TCP connection establishment happens.
________________________________________
16.4 TCP connection establishment
Client sends:
SYN
Proxy responds:
SYN + ACK
Client:
ACK
Conceptually:
Client                         Proxy

   SYN ------------------------>

       <---------------- SYN+ACK

   ACK ------------------------>
Now TCP connection is established.
________________________________________
16.5 The listening socket becomes ready
The kernel has completed enough work that:
FD 3
has an incoming connection waiting to be accepted.
So:
epoll
  ↓
FD 3 ready
Event loop wakes:
epoll_wait()
     ↓
FD 3
     ↓
accept()
________________________________________
16.6 accept() creates another FD
This is important.
The listening FD:
FD 3
doesn't become the client's connection.
Instead:
accept(FD 3)
returns a new FD.
Suppose:
FD 4
Now:
FD 3 → listening socket
FD 4 → Client connection
Visualize:
Proxy

FD 3
 │
 └── Listening socket
       │
       └── accepts connections


FD 4
 │
 └── Client A TCP connection
________________________________________
16.7 Why two FDs?
Because the proxy must continue listening for new clients.
Suppose:
Client A → FD 4
Client B → ?
Client C → ?
The listening socket must remain available:
FD 3 → accept new clients
while:
FD 4 → Client A
handles Client A's data.
________________________________________
16.8 The proxy makes FD 4 non-blocking
Conceptually:
fcntl(FD4, O_NONBLOCK)
Why?
Because the event loop cannot afford:
read(FD4)
   ↓
WAIT
It needs:
read(FD4)
   ↓
data
or:
read(FD4)
   ↓
EAGAIN
________________________________________
16.9 FD 4 is registered with epoll
Now:
epoll
 │
 ├── FD 3 → accept events
 │
 └── FD 4 → client read events
The proxy is ready.
________________________________________
16.10 Client sends HTTP bytes
The client sends:
GET /orders/123 HTTP/1.1
Host: api.example.com
Connection: keep-alive
But TCP transports bytes.
The kernel receives network packets:
Network
   ↓
NIC
   ↓
Linux kernel
   ↓
TCP stack
   ↓
FD 4 receive buffer
Now FD 4 becomes readable.
________________________________________
16.11 epoll wakes the event loop
We get:
epoll_wait()
      ↓
FD 4 READABLE
The event loop finds the connection object:
FD 4
 ↓
Connection object
and calls:
read(FD4)
________________________________________
16.12 The proxy gets bytes—not an HTTP request
Suppose:
read()
returns:
GET /orders/123 HTTP/1.1\r\n
Host: api.example.com\r\n
Maybe the rest hasn't arrived yet.
The proxy therefore stores bytes in an input buffer:
Connection FD 4

Input Buffer
┌──────────────────────────────────────┐
│ GET /orders/123 HTTP/1.1\r\n         │
│ Host: api.example.com\r\n            │
└──────────────────────────────────────┘
It needs to determine:
Has a complete HTTP request arrived?
________________________________________
16.13 HTTP parsing starts
The proxy's HTTP parser examines:
GET /orders/123 HTTP/1.1
It extracts:
Method = GET
Path   = /orders/123
Version = HTTP/1.1
Then headers:
Host: api.example.com
Connection: keep-alive
The parser needs to understand where the request ends.
For a simple GET with no body:
\r\n\r\n
marks the end of the headers.
________________________________________
16.14 Now the proxy has a logical HTTP request
We can represent it as:
Request
├── method: GET
├── path: /orders/123
├── version: HTTP/1.1
├── headers
└── body: empty
This is now an application-level object.
Notice the layers:
Ethernet
   ↓
IP
   ↓
TCP
   ↓
Socket
   ↓
Bytes
   ↓
HTTP parser
   ↓
HTTP Request
Each layer knows different things.
________________________________________
16.15 Now the proxy must decide where to send it
This is where reverse proxy logic begins.
The proxy might have:
/api/orders → backend pool A
/api/users  → backend pool B
/static     → static server
Our request:
/orders/123
might map to:
Order Service
Suppose there are three backend servers:
Backend Pool

10.0.0.11:8080
10.0.0.12:8080
10.0.0.13:8080
The proxy needs to choose one.
________________________________________
16.16 Backend selection
The proxy may use:
Round Robin
Least Connections
Weighted Round Robin
Hash-based selection
Random
Suppose:
Round Robin
selects:
10.0.0.12:8080
Now we have:
Client
   │
   │ Connection A
   ▼
Proxy
   │
   │ Connection B
   ▼
10.0.0.12:8080
________________________________________
16.17 Here's a critical architectural point
The client connection:
Client ↔ Proxy
and backend connection:
Proxy ↔ Backend
are independent.
They have:
different TCP state
different sequence numbers
different buffers
different sockets
different FDs
Potentially:
Client socket FD 4
Backend socket FD 10
________________________________________
16.18 Does the proxy always create a new backend TCP connection?
No.
This is a major production optimization.
The proxy can either:
Option A — Create new backend connection
Client
   │
   ▼
Proxy
   │
   └── TCP connect()
           ↓
        Backend
Option B — Reuse an existing connection
Proxy
  │
  ├── Backend connection #1
  ├── Backend connection #2
  ├── Backend connection #3
  └── Backend connection #4
This is called connection pooling / keep-alive reuse.
We'll go deep into this later.
For now, assume a backend connection needs to be established.
________________________________________
16.19 Proxy calls connect()
The proxy creates:
Backend socket
Suppose:
FD 10
Then:
connect(FD10, 10.0.0.12:8080)
Because this is a non-blocking proxy, connect() may not finish immediately.
This is important.
________________________________________
16.20 Non-blocking connect
A blocking model:
connect()
   ↓
WAIT
   ↓
connected
would block the event-loop thread.
Instead:
connect()
   ↓
connection in progress
   ↓
return control
The proxy can monitor the socket for connection completion.
Conceptually:
FD 10
   ↓
CONNECTING
Then:
epoll
   ↓
FD 10 becomes writable
The proxy can verify whether the connection succeeded.
________________________________________
16.21 Why writable?
This surprises many developers.
For a non-blocking TCP connect, completion is commonly detected through write/error readiness.
The important idea:
connect()
   ↓
in progress
   ↓
event indicates completion
   ↓
check socket error state
Don't simply assume:
WRITABLE = SUCCESS
The connection may have failed.
________________________________________
16.22 Now backend connection is established
We have:
FD 4
Client ↔ Proxy

FD 10
Proxy ↔ Backend
The proxy's internal state might look like:
Connection Object
├── clientFd = 4
├── upstreamFd = 10
├── request
├── inputBuffer
├── outputBuffer
├── state
└── backendServer
This is where the proxy begins coordinating two sockets.
________________________________________
16.23 Proxy forwards the HTTP request
It creates bytes for the backend:
GET /orders/123 HTTP/1.1
Host: api.example.com
...
Then:
write(FD10)
Those bytes go:
User space
   ↓
Kernel socket send buffer
   ↓
TCP
   ↓
Network
   ↓
Backend
Again:
write() does not necessarily mean the backend application has already received or processed the request.
It usually means the bytes have been accepted into the local kernel's sending path/buffer.
This distinction matters enormously for latency reasoning.
________________________________________
16.24 Backend receives the request
Backend machine:
Network
   ↓
NIC
   ↓
Kernel
   ↓
TCP socket
   ↓
Backend process
   ↓
HTTP parser
Now the Java server sees:
GET /orders/123
The application processes it.
For example:
Controller
   ↓
Service
   ↓
Database
This part belongs to backend architecture rather than reverse proxy internals.
________________________________________
16.25 Backend response begins
Suppose backend produces:
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "id": 123,
  "status": "SHIPPED"
}
Backend sends bytes through its socket.
They travel:
Backend
   ↓
TCP
   ↓
Network
   ↓
Proxy NIC
   ↓
Proxy kernel
   ↓
Proxy upstream socket FD 10
FD 10 becomes readable.
________________________________________
16.26 epoll again
The proxy's event loop:
epoll_wait()
      ↓
FD 10 READABLE
It reads:
read(FD10)
Now the proxy has backend response bytes.
Where do they go?
Into an output buffer associated with the client connection.
Conceptually:
Backend FD 10
      │
      │ read()
      ▼
Proxy memory
      │
      ▼
Client output buffer
      │
      ▼
Client FD 4
________________________________________
16.27 Why does buffering matter?
Suppose backend produces data faster than the client can consume it.
Example:
Backend
  ↓
100 MB/s
but:
Client
  ↓
5 MB/s
The proxy can't blindly keep reading forever.
Otherwise:
Backend → Proxy memory
                 ↓
             memory grows
                 ↓
              OOM risk
This leads to one of the most important concepts in networking:
Backpressure
________________________________________
16.28 Backpressure
The proxy needs to regulate:
producer
   ↓
proxy
   ↓
consumer
Here:
Backend = producer
Client = consumer
If:
producer speed > consumer speed
then the proxy must eventually slow the producer.
Conceptually:
Backend
   ↓
Proxy buffer
   ↓
Client
When buffer becomes too large:
stop / reduce reading from backend
or otherwise apply flow control.
This prevents unlimited memory consumption.
We'll later dedicate a deeper lesson to this.
________________________________________
16.29 Proxy sends response to client
Suppose output buffer contains:
{
  "id": 123,
  "status": "SHIPPED"
}
Proxy tries:
write(FD4)
Maybe all bytes fit.
Then:
output buffer = empty
If only part can be written:
write()
   ↓
partial write
remaining bytes stay in the output buffer.
The proxy watches:
FD4 → WRITABLE
and continues later.
________________________________________
16.30 The full request path
Now let's combine everything:
                     CLIENT
                        │
                        │ TCP
                        ▼
               ┌─────────────────┐
               │ Reverse Proxy   │
               │                 │
               │ Listening FD 3  │
               └────────┬────────┘
                        │
                     accept()
                        │
                        ▼
                    Client FD 4
                        │
                     read()
                        │
                        ▼
                  HTTP Parser
                        │
                        ▼
                  Route Selection
                        │
                        ▼
                 Backend Selection
                        │
                        ▼
                   Backend FD 10
                        │
                     connect()
                        │
                        ▼
                  write(request)
                        │
                        ▼
                     BACKEND
                        │
                    process()
                        │
                  response bytes
                        │
                        ▼
                  read(FD 10)
                        │
                        ▼
                 Output Buffer
                        │
                   write(FD 4)
                        │
                        ▼
                     CLIENT
This is the core reverse proxy lifecycle.
________________________________________
16.31 But where does epoll sit?
Every I/O operation is coordinated by the event loop.
                    epoll_wait()
                         │
          ┌──────────────┼──────────────┐
          ▼              ▼              ▼
      Client FD 4    Backend FD 10   Listen FD 3
       READABLE        READABLE        READABLE
          │              │              │
          ▼              ▼              ▼
        read()         read()         accept()
So the event loop is essentially the orchestrator.
________________________________________
16.32 One event loop can manage both directions
This is a powerful idea.
Suppose:
10,000 clients
and:
2,000 backend connections
You don't necessarily need:
12,000 threads
Instead:
Many FDs
   ↓
epoll
   ↓
few event-loop workers
Each event advances a connection's state.
________________________________________
16.33 Why this architecture is so powerful
Imagine:
Client A → waiting for backend DB
Client B → sending request
Client C → receiving response
Client D → idle keep-alive
Client E → backend connection establishing
A thread-per-connection model might have many threads blocked/waiting.
An event-driven model says:
Client A → nothing to do now
Client B → process
Client C → process
Client D → nothing to do
Client E → process
The CPU is focused on work that can actually progress.
________________________________________
16.34 Reverse proxy is therefore a state machine
This is the deeper architectural insight.
A proxy isn't simply:
receive()
send()
It manages states.
For example:
CLIENT_CONNECTION
       ↓
READING_REQUEST
       ↓
REQUEST_COMPLETE
       ↓
SELECT_BACKEND
       ↓
CONNECTING_UPSTREAM
       ↓
FORWARDING_REQUEST
       ↓
WAITING_FOR_RESPONSE
       ↓
READING_RESPONSE
       ↓
WRITING_RESPONSE
       ↓
RESPONSE_COMPLETE
       ↓
KEEP_ALIVE
At any point:
error
timeout
disconnect
retry
can change the state.
________________________________________
16.35 This explains why reverse proxies are complicated
At first glance:
Client → Proxy → Server
looks trivial.
Internally:
Client socket
    +
HTTP parser
    +
routing
    +
backend pool
    +
upstream socket
    +
event loop
    +
input buffers
    +
output buffers
    +
timeouts
    +
connection reuse
    +
backpressure
    +
failure handling
That's the real system.
________________________________________
16.36 Important: TCP connection ≠ HTTP request
You asked about this earlier, so let's reinforce it from the actual lifecycle.
One TCP connection can carry:
HTTP Request 1
HTTP Response 1

HTTP Request 2
HTTP Response 2

HTTP Request 3
HTTP Response 3
For HTTP/1.1 keep-alive:
TCP Connection
│
├── Request 1
├── Response 1
├── Request 2
├── Response 2
└── Request 3
Therefore:
1 TCP connection
≠
1 HTTP request
This is extremely important for understanding connection pools, load balancing, HTTP/2, and performance.
________________________________________
16.37 What happens after the response?
The proxy has a decision.
If:
Connection: close
then:
response complete
      ↓
close socket
If keep-alive:
response complete
      ↓
keep client FD open
      ↓
wait for next request
So the lifecycle can return:
WRITING_RESPONSE
      ↓
RESPONSE_COMPLETE
      ↓
KEEP_ALIVE
      ↓
READING_NEXT_REQUEST
This means a single client socket can live for a long time.
________________________________________
16.38 And this creates an interesting problem
Suppose:
Client connects
and then does nothing.
The connection remains open.
Imagine:
100,000 idle connections
They consume resources:
FDs
memory
connection state
kernel buffers
proxy state
Therefore proxies need:
idle timeout
request timeout
connect timeout
read timeout
write timeout
These will become very important later when we study resilience.
________________________________________
16.39 One request's complete timeline
Let's compress everything:
T0
Client connects
 ↓
TCP handshake

T1
accept()
 ↓
Client FD created

T2
Client sends HTTP bytes
 ↓
kernel receive buffer

T3
epoll reports READABLE
 ↓
read()

T4
HTTP parser
 ↓
request complete

T5
route request
 ↓
select backend

T6
connect backend
 ↓
backend FD

T7
write request upstream
 ↓
backend processes

T8
backend sends response
 ↓
upstream FD READABLE

T9
read response
 ↓
proxy output buffer

T10
client FD WRITABLE
 ↓
write response

T11
response complete
 ↓
keep-alive OR close
That is one complete request lifecycle.
________________________________________
16.40 The most important mental model from Lesson 16
Don't visualize a reverse proxy as:
Client
   ↓
Proxy
   ↓
Backend
Visualize it as:
               REVERSE PROXY
                     │
             ┌───────┴────────┐
             │                │
             ▼                ▼
        Client Socket    Upstream Socket
             │                │
             ▼                ▼
         Input Buffer     Input Buffer
             │                │
             └───────┬────────┘
                     │
                Event Loop
                     │
                  epoll
                     │
                     ▼
              Connection State
The proxy is coordinating two independent asynchronous streams.
That's the real internal architecture.
________________________________________
Lesson 16 checkpoint
You should now be able to answer:
1. Why does accept() return a new FD?
Because:
listening FD
must remain available to accept future connections, while the new FD represents the individual client connection.
2. Is the client TCP connection the same as the backend TCP connection?
No.
Client ↔ Proxy
and:
Proxy ↔ Backend
are independent TCP connections.
3. Does write() mean the backend application has processed the request?
No.
It generally means the bytes were accepted into the local kernel's sending path/buffer; network delivery and application processing happen later.
4. Why does the proxy need output buffers?
Because writes can be partial:
wanted: 1 MB
written: 200 KB
remaining: 824 KB
5. Why does the proxy need backpressure?
Because:
Backend faster than Client
can otherwise cause:
unbounded proxy buffering
→ memory exhaustion

System Design — Lesson 17
Reverse Proxy: Connection Pooling & Keep-Alive Internals
We won't repeat sockets, epoll, EAGAIN, or the basic request lifecycle.
Today we're answering a much more important production question:
If the proxy already connected to a backend once, why should it create another TCP connection for the next HTTP request?
Usually, it shouldn't.
________________________________________
17.1 The problem
Imagine a client sends:
GET /orders/1
The proxy needs to talk to:
Order Service
Naive implementation:
Request 1
Client
   ↓
Proxy
   ↓
TCP connect()
   ↓
Backend
   ↓
response
   ↓
close()

Request 2
Client
   ↓
Proxy
   ↓
TCP connect()
   ↓
Backend
This works.
But it's wasteful.
The proxy is repeatedly paying for:
TCP connection establishment
+
TLS handshake (if HTTPS)
+
kernel/socket setup
+
connection bookkeeping
So we ask:
Can we reuse an existing connection?
Yes.
That's keep-alive + connection pooling.
________________________________________
17.2 First understand TCP connection cost
Suppose:
Proxy → Backend
needs a TCP connection.
TCP establishment involves the handshake:
Proxy                         Backend

   SYN ------------------------>

       <---------------- SYN+ACK

   ACK ------------------------>
Only after that can application data flow normally.
So every new connection has a startup cost.
Now suppose TLS is also involved:
TCP handshake
      ↓
TLS handshake
      ↓
HTTP request
      ↓
HTTP response
That's even more expensive.
________________________________________
17.3 The fundamental optimization
Instead of:
Request 1 → connection 1 → close
Request 2 → connection 2 → close
Request 3 → connection 3 → close
we want:
Connection 1
   │
   ├── Request 1
   ├── Response 1
   ├── Request 2
   ├── Response 2
   ├── Request 3
   └── Response 3
So:
1 TCP connection
        ↓
multiple HTTP requests
This is HTTP keep-alive.
________________________________________
17.4 TCP connection vs HTTP request
This is worth locking into your mental model.
They are different layers:
TCP Connection
│
├── HTTP Request 1
├── HTTP Response 1
├── HTTP Request 2
├── HTTP Response 2
├── HTTP Request 3
└── HTTP Response 3
Therefore:
TCP connection ≠ HTTP request
A connection is a transport-level channel.
HTTP requests are application-level messages traveling through it.
________________________________________
17.5 Client keep-alive and backend keep-alive are separate
A reverse proxy has two sides:
                 Proxy
              /         \
             /           \
        Client          Backend
Therefore there are two independent connection-reuse questions.
Client side
Client ↔ Proxy
Can this TCP connection handle another request?
Backend side
Proxy ↔ Backend
Can this TCP connection be reused for another backend request?
These are independent.
This is an important production concept.
________________________________________
17.6 Example
Suppose:
Client A
keeps a connection open:
Client A ↔ Proxy
The proxy might use:
Backend connection #7
for request 1.
After request 1 finishes:
Backend connection #7
is returned to the pool.
Later:
Client A
sends request 2.
The proxy can take:
Backend connection #7
again.
So:
Client connection
       │
       │ Request 1
       ▼
Backend connection #7
       │
       ▼
      pool

       │
       │ Request 2
       ▼
Backend connection #7
________________________________________
17.7 Now we need a connection pool
A production proxy may have thousands of backend connections.
Conceptually:
Backend: Order Service

Connection Pool
┌────────────────────────────┐
│ Conn 1  IDLE               │
│ Conn 2  BUSY               │
│ Conn 3  IDLE               │
│ Conn 4  BUSY               │
│ Conn 5  IDLE               │
└────────────────────────────┘
The pool manages these connections.
A request arrives.
The proxy asks:
"Give me an available connection to Order Service."
Pool returns:
Conn 3
The proxy uses it.
When finished:
Conn 3 → IDLE
and puts it back into the pool.
________________________________________
17.8 The pool is really a resource manager
Think beyond networking.
It's the same idea as:
Database Connection Pool
Thread Pool
Object Pool
HTTP Client Connection Pool
The fundamental pattern is:
Resource
   ↓
Create
   ↓
Pool
   ↓
Acquire
   ↓
Use
   ↓
Release
   ↓
Pool
The pool exists because creating resources is expensive.
________________________________________
17.9 A simplified pool data structure
A conceptual implementation could use:
class ConnectionPool {

    Queue<Connection> idleConnections;

    Set<Connection> activeConnections;

    Connection acquire() {
        ...
    }

    void release(Connection connection) {
        ...
    }
}
Conceptually:
idleConnections
      ↓
[ C1 ][ C3 ][ C7 ]

activeConnections
      ↓
[ C2 ][ C4 ]
This is not how every production proxy implements it internally, but it's a useful mental model.
________________________________________
17.10 What happens when a request arrives?
Suppose:
Order request
arrives.
The proxy:
1. Find backend pool
2. Try to acquire idle connection
3. Validate connection state
4. Use connection
5. Send request
6. Read response
7. Return connection to pool
So:
Request
   ↓
Backend Pool
   ↓
Acquire connection
   ↓
Use
   ↓
Response complete
   ↓
Release
   ↓
Idle pool
________________________________________
17.11 But there's a hidden problem
What if the pool has:
C1
C2
C3
and all are busy?
Pool
├── C1 BUSY
├── C2 BUSY
└── C3 BUSY
A new request arrives.
What should happen?
We cannot simply create unlimited connections.
Because:
100 requests
→ 100 connections
might be okay.
But:
100,000 requests
→ 100,000 backend connections
could destroy the backend.
________________________________________
17.12 Maximum connections
So pools normally have a limit:
maxConnections = 100
Now:
Connections
0 ─────────────────── 100
                     ↑
                   limit
Once 100 connections are active:
new request
    ↓
pool exhausted
The proxy must decide what to do.
Possible behaviors:
wait
queue
timeout
reject
fail fast
This is a major design decision.
________________________________________
17.13 Pool exhaustion is not merely a connection problem
Consider:
Client requests
       ↓
Proxy
       ↓
Connection Pool
       ↓
Backend
Suppose backend becomes slow.
Requests stay active longer.
Therefore:
connections stay BUSY longer
Then:
pool occupancy ↑
Eventually:
pool full
Then requests wait.
Then latency increases.
Then upstream requests pile up.
Then more resources become occupied.
This can create a cascading failure.
We'll later connect this directly to:
Timeout
Circuit Breaker
Bulkhead
Rate Limiting
________________________________________
17.14 Connection pool as a bulkhead
Here's an important connection.
Suppose Order Service has:
max 100 backend connections
Then one service can't consume unlimited connections.
This can act as a form of isolation:
Order Service
    ↓
100 connections maximum
Other services may have their own pools:
User Service
    ↓
50 connections

Payment Service
    ↓
30 connections
So one backend cannot automatically consume the entire proxy's connection resources.
Later, when we study Bulkhead, you'll see this idea again at a more general level.
________________________________________
17.15 Idle connections
Now imagine:
Pool:

C1 IDLE
C2 IDLE
C3 IDLE
...
C50 IDLE
Why keep all 50 open forever?
Each connection consumes resources.
At some point the proxy should remove unused connections.
So we need:
idle timeout
Example:
idleTimeout = 30 seconds
If:
connection last used = 40 seconds ago
the proxy may close it.
Conceptually:
IDLE
 ↓
30 seconds
 ↓
still unused
 ↓
CLOSE
________________________________________
17.16 Why not keep idle connections forever?
Because the backend may have its own limits.
Suppose:
Proxy
1000 idle connections
      ↓
Backend
max 500 connections
You don't want the proxy consuming backend connection capacity unnecessarily.
There are also:
FD memory
kernel socket buffers
TLS state
application connection state
to consider.
________________________________________
17.17 The stale connection problem
This is one of the most important real-world issues.
Suppose:
Proxy ←──────── TCP connection ───────→ Backend
The connection sits idle.
Proxy thinks:
"Great. I'll reuse this."
But backend has already closed it.
Maybe because of:
backend idle timeout
network failure
server restart
load balancer timeout
firewall/NAT timeout
Now the proxy's pool contains:
C7 = looks idle
but it's actually dead/stale.
________________________________________
17.18 Then a new request arrives
Proxy:
acquire C7
tries:
write(request)
and gets an error.
Now:
request
   ↓
stale connection
   ↓
write failure
The proxy must:
1. remove stale connection
2. create another connection
3. retry if safe/appropriate
But the retry decision is not automatically safe.
This becomes very important when we study idempotency and retries.
________________________________________
17.19 Why can't we blindly retry?
Suppose:
POST /payments
Proxy sends it.
Backend processes the payment.
But response gets lost.
Proxy sees:
connection failure
It might think:
"Let's retry!"
Now the payment could be processed twice.
So:
connection reuse
        ↓
failure
        ↓
retry
        ↓
idempotency problem
This is one of the cross-topic connections we'll eventually study in depth.
________________________________________
17.20 Connection lifetime
A production connection often has multiple limits.
For example:
max idle time
max lifetime
max requests per connection
Conceptually:
Connection
   │
   ├── idle timeout
   ├── max lifetime
   └── max request count
Why max lifetime?
Because long-lived connections can accumulate issues:
stale network paths
load balancer state
NAT state
resource fragmentation
server-side changes
Rotating connections can sometimes improve stability.
________________________________________
17.21 Connection reuse improves latency
Compare:
Without reuse
Request
 ↓
TCP handshake
 ↓
request
 ↓
response
 ↓
close
With reuse
Existing TCP connection
 ↓
request
 ↓
response
The connection establishment overhead disappears for subsequent requests.
If TLS is involved, the savings can be even more significant.
________________________________________
17.22 Connection reuse improves throughput
Suppose backend supports:
10,000 requests/sec
but connection creation is expensive.
If every request requires a new connection:
10,000 requests/sec
→ 10,000 TCP connections/sec created
That's enormous churn.
With pooling:
100 connections
→ thousands of requests
can flow through those connections over time.
This dramatically reduces connection-management overhead.
________________________________________
17.23 But pooling isn't free
Here's the important trade-off.
Suppose:
maxConnections = 1000
You might think:
More connections = more performance.
Not necessarily.
Each connection can consume:
memory
FD
kernel buffers
CPU
TLS state
backend resources
Too many connections can create:
context switching
contention
memory pressure
backend overload
So there is an optimal region.
Not:
MAX_CONNECTIONS = INFINITY
________________________________________
17.24 Connection pool sizing
Imagine:
Backend
   │
   └── 8 CPU cores
You configure:
10,000 connections
Does that mean the backend can process 10,000 requests simultaneously?
No.
The number of connections and amount of useful concurrency are different concepts.
You must consider:
CPU
DB connections
thread pools
downstream services
request latency
I/O
memory
For example:
10,000 clients
       ↓
Proxy
       ↓
100 backend connections
       ↓
Backend
       ↓
DB pool = 30
The actual bottleneck might be:
DB pool = 30
not the proxy.
________________________________________
17.25 The bottleneck moves
This is a core system-design principle.
Imagine:
1000 clients
    ↓
Load Balancer
    ↓
Reverse Proxy
    ↓
Backend
    ↓
Database
Suppose you increase proxy connections:
100 → 500
You might improve proxy throughput.
But then:
Backend → Database
gets overloaded.
So:
Improving one layer
        ↓
moves pressure to another layer
That's why performance tuning must examine the entire system.
________________________________________
17.26 A simplified connection pool algorithm
Conceptually:
acquire():

    if idle connection exists:
        connection = removeIdle()
        
        if connection is healthy:
            markActive(connection)
            return connection

        close(connection)

    if activeCount < maxConnections:
        connection = createConnection()
        markActive(connection)
        return connection

    wait / queue / timeout / reject
Then:
release(connection):

    if connection is healthy
       and reusable:
        markIdle(connection)
        addToIdlePool(connection)
    else:
        close(connection)
This is the basic mechanism.
________________________________________
17.27 Important: "release" doesn't necessarily mean close
This distinction is critical.
A naive developer may think:
connection.close();
after each request.
A pool means:
release(connection)
does not necessarily mean:
TCP close
It means:
"Return this resource to the pool."
The pool owns its lifecycle.
________________________________________
17.28 Connection pool state machine
A useful mental model:
             create
               │
               ▼
           CONNECTING
               │
            success
               ▼
             IDLE
               │
            acquire
               ▼
             ACTIVE
               │
          request done
               ▼
             IDLE
               │
       ┌───────┴────────┐
       │                │
   idle timeout      error
       │                │
       ▼                ▼
     CLOSED           CLOSED
This is much closer to how you should mentally model connection pooling.
________________________________________
17.29 What happens when pool is exhausted?
Let's examine four possibilities.
Option 1 — Wait
Request
 ↓
pool full
 ↓
wait
 ↓
connection released
 ↓
request gets connection
Advantage:
requests aren't immediately rejected
Problem:
waiting requests accumulate
________________________________________
Option 2 — Timeout
pool full
 ↓
wait
 ↓
timeout
 ↓
failure
This prevents indefinite waiting.
________________________________________
Option 3 — Reject immediately
pool full
 ↓
fail fast
This protects the system but increases errors.
________________________________________
Option 4 — Create more connections
This can work only up to a carefully chosen limit.
Otherwise:
pool full
 ↓
create more
 ↓
backend overloaded
 ↓
backend slows
 ↓
more connections required
 ↓
backend collapses
This is a positive feedback loop.
________________________________________
17.30 The dangerous feedback loop
This is worth remembering.
Backend becomes slow
       ↓
Requests take longer
       ↓
Connections remain busy longer
       ↓
Pool fills
       ↓
Requests wait
       ↓
Latency increases
       ↓
More requests remain in system
       ↓
Backend gets more pressure
       ↓
Backend becomes even slower
This is a queueing / overload spiral.
This is why later concepts like:
Timeout
Rate Limiting
Circuit Breaker
Bulkhead
Load Shedding
exist.
________________________________________
17.31 Connection pooling and Little's Law
Now let's introduce one useful performance relationship.
A basic queueing relationship is:
L = λ × W
Where:
L = average number of requests in system
λ = throughput
W = average time spent in system
Suppose:
λ = 1000 requests/sec
W = 100 ms = 0.1 sec
Then:
L = 1000 × 0.1
  = 100
Approximately 100 requests are in-flight.
If latency becomes:
W = 1 second
then:
L = 1000 × 1
  = 1000
Now approximately 1000 requests are in-flight.
This explains something very important:
When downstream latency increases, required concurrency increases even if traffic volume stays constant.
________________________________________
17.32 Why this matters to connection pools
Suppose:
1000 requests/sec
and average backend latency:
50 ms
Approximate concurrency:
1000 × 0.05 = 50
But backend latency becomes:
500 ms
Then:
1000 × 0.5 = 500
Now you may need approximately:
500 concurrent backend operations
to sustain the same throughput.
So latency directly influences connection requirements.
________________________________________
17.33 This is why "just increase the connection pool" is dangerous
Suppose:
Pool = 100
and requests are slow.
You increase:
100 → 1000
Maybe throughput improves temporarily.
But if the backend is slow because the database is overloaded:
Proxy
 ↓
1000 connections
 ↓
Backend
 ↓
Database overloaded
you've amplified the problem.
The correct solution may be:
fix DB
reduce traffic
cache
rate-limit
timeout
bulkhead
not:
increase connections
________________________________________
17.34 Client-side pool vs server-side pool
Be precise about where the pool lives.
In our architecture:
Client
   ↓
Reverse Proxy
   ↓
Backend
The proxy owns backend connections.
But the backend application may itself have:
Database connection pool
So:
Proxy
   │
   │ HTTP connection pool
   ▼
Java Service
   │
   │ JDBC connection pool
   ▼
Database
There are multiple pools in the system.
________________________________________
17.35 Pool multiplication
This creates another interesting problem.
Suppose:
10 proxy instances
and each has:
max 100 backend connections
Potential total:
10 × 100 = 1000 backend connections
Now imagine:
100 application instances
and each has:
DB pool = 50
Potential DB connections:
100 × 50 = 5000
This is why distributed systems must reason about aggregate capacity, not just per-instance configuration.
________________________________________
17.36 Connection pool architecture
A more realistic conceptual structure:
                 Reverse Proxy
                       │
              Backend Pool Map
                       │
       ┌───────────────┼───────────────┐
       ▼               ▼               ▼
 Order Service    User Service    Payment Service
       │               │               │
       ▼               ▼               ▼
    Pool A           Pool B           Pool C
       │
   ┌───┼────┐
   ▼   ▼    ▼
  C1  C2   C3
The proxy doesn't necessarily have one giant pool.
It needs to associate connections with a backend target.
________________________________________
17.37 Why backend identity matters
Suppose:
Order Service:
10.0.0.11
10.0.0.12
10.0.0.13
A connection to:
10.0.0.11
cannot simply be reused for:
10.0.0.12
The connection belongs to a particular destination.
So the pool may be organized around:
Backend endpoint
or:
Backend cluster + destination
This becomes more interesting when service discovery and load balancing enter the picture.
________________________________________
17.38 Connection reuse and load balancing
Suppose the backend pool contains:
A
B
C
and the proxy creates:
10 connections to A
10 connections to B
10 connections to C
Now request distribution isn't simply:
Request 1 → A
Request 2 → B
Request 3 → C
The proxy is choosing among available backend connections.
This is one reason:
Connection reuse interacts with load-balancing strategy.
Later we'll examine this in detail.
________________________________________
17.39 HTTP/1.1 vs HTTP/2
Keep-alive behaves differently depending on the HTTP version.
With HTTP/1.1:
TCP connection
   ↓
Request
   ↓
Response
   ↓
Request
   ↓
Response
One connection can be reused sequentially.
HTTP/2 changes the model significantly.
One TCP connection can carry multiple concurrent streams:
             TCP Connection
                   │
       ┌───────────┼───────────┐
       ▼           ▼           ▼
   Stream 1    Stream 2    Stream 3
   Request A   Request B   Request C
So HTTP/2 introduces multiplexing.
This drastically changes connection-pool design.
We will cover this later rather than mixing it into today's lesson.
________________________________________
17.40 Important distinction
Don't confuse:
Keep-alive
with:
Connection pooling
They're related but not identical.
Keep-alive
Means:
Don't close the connection after one request/response.
Connection pooling
Means:
Maintain reusable connections as managed resources and allow multiple requests to acquire/release them.
A pool generally relies on persistent connections, but pooling is the broader resource-management mechanism.
________________________________________
17.41 Performance summary
Connection reuse gives us:
Less TCP handshake overhead
Less TLS handshake overhead
Less socket creation
Less connection churn
Lower latency
Higher throughput
But introduces:
Pool management
Stale connections
Idle connection cleanup
Pool exhaustion
Resource limits
Fairness issues
Failure handling
So once again:
Optimization
     ↓
new complexity
That's a recurring theme throughout system design.
________________________________________
17.42 Production mental model
At this point, visualize the proxy like this:
                         REVERSE PROXY
                               │
                 ┌─────────────┴─────────────┐
                 │                           │
             Client Side                Backend Side
                 │                           │
         Client connections            Connection pools
                 │                           │
                 │                   ┌───────┼───────┐
                 │                   ▼       ▼       ▼
                 │                 Pool A  Pool B  Pool C
                 │                   │
                 │                 C1 C2 C3
                 │
                 └─────────────┬─────────────┘
                               │
                          Event Loop
                               │
                             epoll
And each backend connection has a lifecycle:
CREATE
  ↓
CONNECT
  ↓
IDLE
  ↓
ACQUIRE
  ↓
ACTIVE
  ↓
RELEASE
  ↓
IDLE
  ↓
TIMEOUT / ERROR
  ↓
CLOSE
________________________________________
17.43 The deeper lesson
Connection pooling isn't really about HTTP.
It's about expensive resources with limited capacity.
The pattern appears everywhere:
TCP connections
Database connections
Threads
File descriptors
Memory buffers
GPU workers
Connection pools
The general design problem is:
How many resources should we create, how do we reuse them, and what happens when demand exceeds capacity?
That question will repeatedly appear throughout our system-design journey.
________________________________________
17.44 What you should be able to reason about now
Suppose someone says:
"Our service has high latency. Increase the HTTP connection pool from 100 to 1000."
You should not immediately agree.
You should ask:
Why is latency high?

Is the pool exhausted?

Are connections actually being reused?

Is the backend slow?

Is the DB slow?

Are connections waiting?

Are stale connections causing failures?

Is the backend itself limited to 200 connections?

Are we creating too many concurrent requests?

Is there a queue forming?

What's the downstream bottleneck?
That is the mindset we're building.
________________________________________
Lesson 17 — Core takeaway
Remember this chain:
New connection per request
        ↓
TCP/TLS establishment overhead
        ↓
High latency + connection churn
Therefore:
Keep-alive
        ↓
Reuse TCP connections
        ↓
Connection Pool
        ↓
Acquire → Use → Release
        ↓
Limit resources
        ↓
Idle timeout + max lifetime
        ↓
Handle stale connections
        ↓
Handle pool exhaustion
And the most important production insight:
A connection pool is not just a performance optimization. It is a concurrency-control and resource-protection mechanism.
