Lesson 19 — API Gateway Internals
Part 1 — Why API Gateway Exists
We now move forward.
First question:
Why did API Gateway become necessary if we already have a Load Balancer and Reverse Proxy?
This distinction is extremely important.
________________________________________
19.1 Start with a simple system
Imagine an application with one backend:
Client
   ↓
Load Balancer
   ↓
Order Service
Simple.
The load balancer's main concern is:
"Which server instance should receive this request?"
For example:
                 Load Balancer
                 /     |     \
                ↓      ↓      ↓
             Order-1 Order-2 Order-3
Its responsibility is primarily traffic distribution.
________________________________________
19.2 Now the company grows
Our application becomes microservices:
                   Clients
                      │
        ┌─────────────┼─────────────┐
        ↓             ↓             ↓
    Web App       Mobile App     Partner App
And backend becomes:
             Microservices

          Order Service
               │
          Payment Service
               │
           User Service
               │
        Inventory Service
               │
       Notification Service
Now the client needs to communicate with many services.
The naive architecture becomes:
Client
  │
  ├──→ Order Service
  │
  ├──→ Payment Service
  │
  ├──→ User Service
  │
  ├──→ Inventory Service
  │
  └──→ Notification Service
This creates a problem.
________________________________________
19.3 The client now knows too much
Suppose the mobile application needs to display:
Order Details
To construct the page it needs:
User information
Order information
Product information
Payment status
Shipping information
The mobile app might make:
GET /users/123
GET /orders/456
GET /products/789
GET /payments/456
GET /shipping/456
That's already 5 calls.
The client now needs to understand:
where each service lives
which API belongs to which service
authentication requirements
versioning
timeouts
retries
service failures
response formats
This creates client-side coupling.
________________________________________
19.4 The architecture gets ugly
Imagine:
                         Mobile App
                             │
             ┌───────────────┼────────────────┐
             │               │                │
             ▼               ▼                ▼
        User Service    Order Service    Payment Service
             │               │                │
             ▼               ▼                ▼
        Inventory       Shipping        Notification
Now imagine 50 microservices.
The client becomes aware of:
50 service endpoints
50 authentication policies
50 versions
50 failure behaviors
That's terrible architecture.
We need a boundary.
________________________________________
19.5 Introduce API Gateway
Now:
                         Client
                            │
                            ▼
                     ┌─────────────┐
                     │ API Gateway │
                     └──────┬──────┘
                            │
          ┌─────────────────┼─────────────────┐
          ▼                 ▼                 ▼
      User Service      Order Service    Payment Service
The client knows only:
api.company.com
The gateway knows:
User Service
Order Service
Payment Service
Inventory Service
...
This is the first major reason API gateways exist.
The API Gateway creates a stable external API boundary around a changing internal service topology.
________________________________________
19.6 But here's the deeper problem
A gateway isn't merely:
Client → Gateway → Service
It can become a policy enforcement and request orchestration layer.
For example:
Client
   ↓
API Gateway
   │
   ├── Authentication
   ├── Authorization
   ├── Routing
   ├── Rate limiting
   ├── Request validation
   ├── TLS termination
   ├── Logging
   ├── Metrics
   ├── Tracing
   ├── API versioning
   └── Transformation
   │
   ▼
Microservices
Now you can see why it is a separate system-design component.
________________________________________
19.7 API Gateway vs Reverse Proxy
This is an important distinction.
A reverse proxy can do:
Client
  ↓
Reverse Proxy
  ↓
Backend
Its primary job is traffic forwarding and infrastructure-level handling.
An API Gateway is more API-aware.
For example:
GET /api/v1/orders/123
Gateway might understand:
/v1/orders
means:
Order Service
and:
/v1/users
means:
User Service
It can apply API-specific policies.
________________________________________
19.8 Don't think of Gateway as "just another proxy"
Think in layers:
                 Client
                    │
                    ▼
             API Gateway
                    │
       ┌────────────┼────────────┐
       │            │            │
 Authentication   Routing     Policies
       │            │            │
       └────────────┼────────────┘
                    ▼
             Reverse Proxy
                    │
                    ▼
             Load Balancing
                    │
                    ▼
              Service
In real implementations, these responsibilities can overlap and may be combined into one product.
But conceptually, keeping the layers separate helps you reason correctly.
________________________________________
19.9 API Gateway request flow
Let's follow one request internally.
Client sends:
GET /api/v1/orders/123
Authorization: Bearer <token>
The request reaches:
Client
   ↓
Network
   ↓
API Gateway
Now the gateway processes it.
Conceptually:
1. Accept connection
2. Parse HTTP request
3. Determine route
4. Authenticate
5. Authorize
6. Apply policies
7. Determine backend
8. Forward request
9. Receive response
10. Transform response if necessary
11. Return response
We'll break these down individually.
________________________________________
19.10 Step 1 — Request parsing
Gateway receives bytes.
At the network level:
TCP
 ↓
bytes
HTTP parser turns those bytes into something conceptually like:
Method = GET

Path = /api/v1/orders/123

Headers:
    Authorization = ...
    Accept = application/json
Internally the gateway needs request structures.
Conceptually:
class HttpRequest {
    HttpMethod method;
    String path;
    Headers headers;
    Body body;
}
The actual implementation is considerably more optimized.
________________________________________
19.11 Step 2 — Route matching
Gateway now asks:
Which backend should handle /api/v1/orders/123?
It might have routing configuration:
/api/v1/users/**       → User Service
/api/v1/orders/**      → Order Service
/api/v1/payments/**    → Payment Service
/api/v1/products/**    → Product Service
The gateway performs route matching.
Conceptually:
Request path
     ↓
Routing table
     ↓
Matching rule
     ↓
Backend target
________________________________________
19.12 Internally, routing is a lookup problem
Suppose we have:
/api/users
/api/orders
/api/payments
/api/products
A naive implementation might scan every rule:
for route in routes:
    if route.matches(request):
        return route.backend;
That's:
O(N)
where N = number of routes.
For a gateway with thousands of routes, that can become inefficient.
So high-performance gateways can use specialized routing structures such as:
Trie
Radix Tree
Prefix Tree
Hash Map
Compiled routing structures
This is exactly the type of internal implementation detail we'll study.
________________________________________
19.13 Example: Trie-based routing
Imagine routes:
/api/users
/api/orders
/api/products
A conceptual tree:
/
└── api
     ├── users
     ├── orders
     └── products
Request:
/api/orders/123
walks:
/
 ↓
api
 ↓
orders
 ↓
123
The gateway can identify:
Order Service
without scanning unrelated routes.
________________________________________
19.14 Step 3 — Authentication
Now the gateway might inspect:
Authorization: Bearer eyJ...
It needs to determine:
Who is this caller?
Possible mechanisms:
JWT
OAuth2
API keys
mTLS
session tokens
Conceptually:
Request
   ↓
Authentication
   ↓
Identity
For example:
userId = 123
________________________________________
19.15 Why put authentication at the gateway?
Without centralized authentication:
Client
   ↓
User Service → authenticate
Client
   ↓
Order Service → authenticate
Client
   ↓
Payment Service → authenticate
Every service repeats infrastructure logic.
With gateway:
Client
   ↓
Gateway
   ↓
Authenticate
   ↓
Service
Now services can focus more on business logic.
But there is an important architectural caveat:
Gateway authentication does not automatically eliminate service-level authorization.
Suppose the gateway knows:
user = Gopal
But Order Service must still determine:
Is Gopal allowed to access order 123?
That's a business authorization decision.
So:
Authentication ≠ Authorization
We'll go much deeper into this later when discussing security architecture.
________________________________________
19.16 Step 4 — Authorization
Suppose the authenticated identity is:
user = 123
role = CUSTOMER
Request:
DELETE /api/v1/orders/456
Gateway policy might say:
CUSTOMER cannot call admin APIs
So the gateway can reject:
403 Forbidden
before the request reaches the backend.
This protects backend capacity.
________________________________________
19.17 Step 5 — Policy enforcement
Now the gateway can enforce cross-cutting policies.
Examples:
maximum request size
allowed HTTP methods
allowed content types
API version
rate limit
IP rules
authentication
authorization
timeouts
This creates an important property:
                  Gateway
                     │
          ┌──────────┼──────────┐
          ▼          ▼          ▼
       Client A   Client B   Client C
          │          │          │
          └──────────┼──────────┘
                     ▼
              Common policies
Instead of implementing the same policy independently in every service.
________________________________________
19.18 Step 6 — Determine backend
After policy processing:
/api/v1/orders/123
might become:
Order Service
But now we have another layer.
There might be multiple instances:
Order Service
     │
 ┌───┼────┐
 ▼   ▼    ▼
O1  O2   O3
The gateway may use:
service discovery
load balancing
health status
connection pooling
to choose the actual instance.
This is where the Gateway, Reverse Proxy, and Load Balancer concepts connect.
________________________________________
19.19 The full conceptual stack
Client
   │
   ▼
┌─────────────────────┐
│ API Gateway         │
│                     │
│ Auth                │
│ Authorization       │
│ Routing             │
│ Rate Limit          │
│ Validation          │
│ API Versioning      │
└──────────┬──────────┘
           │
           ▼
┌─────────────────────┐
│ Reverse Proxy       │
│                     │
│ Connection Pool     │
│ HTTP forwarding     │
└──────────┬──────────┘
           │
           ▼
┌─────────────────────┐
│ Load Balancing       │
└──────────┬──────────┘
           │
       ┌───┼───┐
       ▼   ▼   ▼
      O1  O2  O3
Again, a real product may combine several of these layers.
We're separating them conceptually to understand responsibilities.
________________________________________
19.20 The most important architectural reason
Microservices introduce internal complexity.
The client should not need to know that complexity.
So:
WITHOUT GATEWAY

Client
 ├── User Service
 ├── Order Service
 ├── Payment Service
 ├── Inventory Service
 ├── Shipping Service
 └── Notification Service
becomes:
WITH GATEWAY

Client
      │
      ▼
API Gateway
      │
 ┌────┼─────┬──────┬───────┐
 ▼    ▼     ▼      ▼       ▼
User Order Payment Inventory Shipping
The gateway acts as a boundary between external API consumers and internal service topology.
That's the key mental model.
________________________________________
19.21 But we have created a new problem
Look carefully:
Client
   ↓
API Gateway
   ↓
Services
Every request now passes through the gateway.
What happens if:
Gateway crashes?
Potentially:
ALL CLIENT REQUESTS
        ↓
       FAIL
We've introduced a potential single point of failure.
Therefore production gateways are themselves distributed:
                 DNS / LB
                    │
        ┌───────────┼───────────┐
        ▼           ▼           ▼
       GW1         GW2         GW3
        │           │           │
        └───────────┼───────────┘
                    ▼
               Microservices
This is a critical design principle:
A gateway cannot be a single machine if it is on the critical path of every request.
________________________________________
19.22 Gateway scaling
Suppose:
10,000 requests/sec
One gateway handles:
2,000 requests/sec
We need roughly:
5 gateway instances
plus headroom.
Then:
                 Load Balancer
                      │
          ┌───────────┼───────────┐
          ▼           ▼           ▼
         GW1         GW2         GW3
The gateway itself becomes horizontally scalable.
Now you have:
Load Balancer
      ↓
API Gateway cluster
      ↓
Service cluster
Notice the layering.
________________________________________
19.23 Gateway state becomes dangerous
Suppose gateway instance 1 stores:
session information
rate-limit counters
routing state
authentication state
in local memory.
Then:
Client A
   ↓
GW1
next request:
Client A
   ↓
GW2
GW2 doesn't have GW1's state.
Therefore scalable gateways should minimize local state or use shared/distributed state where necessary.
This is one of the reasons distributed system design becomes difficult.
________________________________________
19.24 API Gateway is a potential bottleneck
Suppose:
Client traffic = 100k req/sec
and gateway processing adds:
2 ms/request
That gateway layer now has a significant computational cost.
Gateway processing might include:
TLS
HTTP parsing
JWT verification
routing
logging
metrics
tracing
rate limiting
serialization
Therefore the gateway itself must be highly optimized.
________________________________________
19.25 Why JWT verification can matter
Suppose every request carries a JWT.
Gateway verifies its signature.
If using asymmetric cryptography:
RSA / ECDSA
verification consumes CPU.
At:
100,000 requests/sec
even a small amount of CPU work becomes significant.
Therefore production architecture may consider:
token caching
key caching
efficient cryptographic libraries
hardware acceleration
gateway scaling
Again:
A cross-cutting feature becomes a system-wide cost when placed on the critical path.
________________________________________
19.26 Gateway aggregation
Here's another powerful feature.
Client needs:
Order
User
Payment
Instead of:
Client
 ├──→ User Service
 ├──→ Order Service
 └──→ Payment Service
the gateway could expose:
GET /customer-dashboard
and internally:
Gateway
   ├──→ User Service
   ├──→ Order Service
   └──→ Payment Service
Then aggregate responses:
             Gateway
          /     |      \
         ↓      ↓       ↓
       User   Order   Payment
         \      |      /
          \     |     /
           Aggregation
                ↓
             Client
This is called API composition / aggregation.
It can reduce client round trips.
But it creates another problem:
The gateway is now doing business-flow orchestration.
That's dangerous if taken too far.
________________________________________
19.27 Gateway becoming a "God service"
Suppose we keep adding:
authentication
authorization
routing
aggregation
business logic
database queries
transformations
payments
order calculations
Eventually:
API Gateway
becomes a giant service containing business logic.
That's an architectural smell.
A healthy gateway generally handles:
cross-cutting API concerns
rather than becoming the application's business brain.
________________________________________
19.28 API Gateway vs BFF
Another architecture we'll encounter later is:
Backend For Frontend
Instead of one generic gateway:
                 Gateway
                /       \
             Mobile     Web
we can have:
                 API Layer
                /         \
               /           \
          Mobile BFF      Web BFF
               │             │
               └──────┬──────┘
                      ▼
                 Microservices
Why?
Mobile and web may need different response shapes.
This reduces client complexity without forcing one gateway API to satisfy every client.
We'll revisit this when discussing API design and microservices architecture.
________________________________________
19.29 The request path we're building
Our architecture is becoming:
Client
  │
  ▼
Load Balancer
  │
  ▼
API Gateway
  │
  ├── Authentication
  ├── Authorization
  ├── Routing
  ├── Validation
  └── Policies
  │
  ▼
Reverse Proxy / HTTP Client
  │
  ├── Connection Pool
  └── Backend Selection
  │
  ▼
Load Balancing
  │
  ▼
Service Instance
We have already learned the pieces individually.
Now we're seeing how they fit together.
________________________________________
19.30 One important correction to our mental model
Don't memorize:
Load Balancer
   ↓
Reverse Proxy
   ↓
API Gateway
as a mandatory physical chain.
That would be wrong.
Real systems can combine these functions.
For example, one technology can simultaneously provide:
TLS termination
reverse proxy
load balancing
API routing
authentication
rate limiting
The important thing is to understand responsibilities, not force every responsibility into a separate machine.
________________________________________
19.31 API Gateway's responsibility boundary
Think of it like this:
          EXTERNAL WORLD
                │
                ▼
        ┌───────────────┐
        │ API Gateway   │
        └───────┬───────┘
                │
       INTERNAL WORLD
                │
       ┌────────┼────────┐
       ▼        ▼        ▼
    User      Order    Payment
The gateway protects the internal architecture from external consumers.
Therefore if internally you change:
Order Service
from:
Order Service V1
to:
Order Service V2
the client ideally shouldn't care.
The gateway can route:
/api/v1/orders → V1
/api/v2/orders → V2
That is another major capability:
API versioning
________________________________________
19.32 Why API versioning belongs at the boundary
Imagine:
Mobile app version 1
Mobile app version 2
Web app
Partner API
They may all expect different contracts.
The gateway can expose:
/v1/orders
/v2/orders
and map those to appropriate internal services.
This allows internal evolution without breaking all clients simultaneously.
________________________________________
19.33 Performance model
At a high level:
Total latency
=
Client → Gateway
+
Gateway processing
+
Gateway → Service
+
Service processing
+
Service → Gateway
+
Gateway → Client
The gateway adds work.
Therefore we want:
Gateway overhead << backend processing time
But if the backend is extremely fast:
Backend = 1 ms
Gateway = 3 ms
the gateway becomes a major part of total latency.
This is why gateway design matters at high scale.
________________________________________
19.34 Failure model
Possible gateway failures:
Gateway process crash
CPU saturation
Memory exhaustion
Connection pool exhaustion
TLS overload
Authentication dependency failure
Routing configuration error
Service discovery failure
Rate-limit state failure
Network failure
Bad deployment
And because gateway is on the critical path:
Gateway failure
      ↓
many APIs fail simultaneously
This is why gateway architecture eventually intersects strongly with:
Timeout
Retry
Circuit Breaker
Bulkhead
Rate Limiting
But we will not jump into those now.
They belong to Module 4.
________________________________________
19.35 The API Gateway mental model
Keep this picture:
                        CLIENTS
                           │
                           ▼
                 ┌──────────────────┐
                 │   API GATEWAY    │
                 │                  │
                 │ Route            │
                 │ Authenticate     │
                 │ Authorize        │
                 │ Validate         │
                 │ Transform        │
                 │ Version          │
                 │ Observe          │
                 └────────┬─────────┘
                          │
              ┌───────────┼────────────┐
              ▼           ▼            ▼
            User        Order        Payment
           Service     Service       Service
The gateway isn't primarily about making services faster.
It's about controlling and shaping access to services.
